'''Arctisca''' (ärk-tis′kä), ''n. pl.'' [NL., dim. of Gr. ἄρκτος, a bear.] The water-bears, or bear-animalcules, otherwise known as the ''Tardigrada'', ''Macrobiotida'', or ''Colpoda'', a group of uncertain value and position, formerly associated with the rotifers, but now usually considered an order of ''Arachnida'', and located in the vicinity of the ''Acarida.'' {{smaller|They are microscopic aquatic creatures, living in moss and wet sand, often in company with rotifers. They have a vermiform body, with four pairs of very short feet terminated by hooked claws, no distinction of cephalothorax and abdomen, and a suctorial mouth with two stylets, resembling that of a tick or mite. The young usually have the same number of legs as the adult. The}}

